1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,790
The red light switches once a second, driven by a hardware alarm clock. Let's read

2
00:00:05,790 --> 00:00:06,500
every line.

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:14,358
Open 06 pit periodic tick reference. Your project needs the pit driver. Paste both

4
00:00:14,358 --> 00:00:14,860
files.

5
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:22,072
Line 89 is the PIT driver, the periodic interrupt timer. The others handle text,

6
00:00:22,072 --> 00:00:23,780
board, pins and clock.

7
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:28,460
Switch on lines 134 to 148.

8
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:33,840
Line 134 gives the handler the official name the chip looks for.

9
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:38,100
Line 135 names the timer's interrupt number.

10
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:42,200
Line 136 asks how fast the timer's clock runs.

11
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:48,540
Lines 138 and 139 nickname the red light: prepare it on, and toggle it.

12
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:55,840
Line 141 is the flag, starting false. Volatile means it can change unexpectedly.

13
00:00:56,840 --> 00:01:00,200
Line 143 runs every time the alarm rings.

14
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:04,380
Line 145 clears the timer's flag for channel zero.

15
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:07,680
Line 147 raises our own flag.

16
00:01:08,180 --> 00:01:12,100
If line 145 were missing, what would happen?

17
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:17,600
Your turn. Pause the video.

18
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:27,120
The chip would think the alarm is still ringing and keep running the handler.

19
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:32,120
Switch on lines 177 to 181.

20
00:01:32,620 --> 00:01:41,618
Lines 177 to 179 ready pins, clock and text. Line 181 prepares the red light,

21
00:01:41,618 --> 00:01:43,020
switched on.

22
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:47,060
Switch on lines 209 to 212.

23
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:53,934
Line 209 makes a settings form. Line 211 fills it with defaults. Line 212 applies it

24
00:01:53,934 --> 00:01:54,920
to the timer.

25
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:58,960
Switch on lines 247 to 251.

26
00:01:59,460 --> 00:02:06,285
Line 247 sets channel zero's period. U sec to count turns one million microseconds

27
00:02:06,285 --> 00:02:07,700
into clock ticks.

28
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:11,720
Line 249 lets the timer raise an interrupt.

29
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:15,420
Line 251 lets the processor hear it. You need both.

30
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:20,020
Switch on lines 278 to 280.

31
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:28,920
Line 278 prints a starting message. Line 280 starts the countdown.

32
00:02:29,420 --> 00:02:30,620
Will the light blink yet?

33
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:36,120
Your turn. Pause the video.

34
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:38,280
No. Nothing reads the flag.

35
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:41,440
Switch on lines 308 to 318.

36
00:02:41,940 --> 00:02:46,580
Line 308 loops forever. Line 310 checks the flag.

37
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:51,880
Line 312 prints a message. The spelling comes from the original example.

38
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:58,460
Line 314 toggles the light. Line 316 lowers the flag for the next tick.

39
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:04,946
Save, build, load and resume. Count ten ticks with a phone timer. It should take

40
00:03:04,946 --> 00:03:06,180
about ten seconds.

41
00:03:07,180 --> 00:03:14,300
On line 247, change a million to 250,000. How many ticks per second now?

42
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:19,800
Your turn. Pause the video.

43
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:20,200
Four.

44
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:26,914
Then try 100,000. Restore one million when you're done. You can now explain a

45
00:03:26,914 --> 00:03:29,980
hardware timer from setup to response.

